+I would begin by marking the hillslopes, their boundaries and positions, the small streams and riparian strips, and the fenced areas. The next decision is whether erosion is being compared **among hillslopes**, **along hillslopes**, or both. Those choices lead to different sampling units and allocations.
+
+For example, if the aim is to compare erosion among hillslopes, the 12 locations could represent 12 separately selected hillslopes, with a clearly defined and comparable measurement at each. If the aim is to understand how erosion changes from the upper to the lower part of a slope, it may be more informative to select four hillslopes and measure three positions on each. That second design has twelve measurement locations but only four hillslopes. A study of slope shape or length might require recording those properties for each hillslope as well. More positions within a hillslope describe that hillslope better; they do not add independent hillslopes.
+
+Slope steepness, vegetation cover, soil type and previous management may all affect erosion. The selection could deliberately cover gentle and steep slopes, then choose locations within those groups using an explicit procedure. Stream margins and riparian strips should be included or excluded according to what the question aims to represent. Fenced areas may require permission; if access remains impossible, describe which parts of the landscape are absent from the sample and how that affects the conclusions.
+
+Remember that the design depends on which differences you want to investigate: among hillslopes, along their length, or in relation to shape and environmental conditions. See [Chapters 5](05-sampling-replication.qmd) and [6](06-space.qmd).
 
